# LeetCode 2913: Subarrays Distinct Element Sum of Squares I

## Problem Statement
Given an integer array `nums`, consider every possible subarray. For each subarray, calculate the number of distinct elements and square that count. Return the sum of these squared values.

## Approach: Brute Force + Hash Set

- Traverse every possible starting index `i` of a subarray.
- Create an `unordered_set` to store distinct elements for each starting index.
- Extend the subarray using index `j`.
- If `nums[j]` is not already present in the set, insert it and increment the distinct element count.
- Add the square of the distinct element count to the total sum for every subarray.
- Return the final sum.

## C++ Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int sumCounts(vector<int>& nums) {
        int Total_count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            unordered_set<int> st;
            int count = 0;

            for (int j = i; j < nums.size(); j++) {
                if (!st.count(nums[j])) {
                    count++;
                    st.insert(nums[j]);
                }

                Total_count += count * count;
            }
        }

        return Total_count;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** `O(n²)` expected, assuming average constant-time hash set operations.
- **Space Complexity:** `O(n)` for the hash set.

Here, `n` is the size of the input array.

## Key Concepts
- Arrays and subarrays
- Nested loops
- `unordered_set` in C++
- Counting distinct elements
- Brute-force enumeration

## Key Learning
By fixing the starting index and extending the subarray one element at a time, we can efficiently maintain the distinct element count without recalculating it for every subarray.
